Cauda equina syndrome is compression of the lumbosacral roots below the conus, usually by a large central disc prolapse. The national GIRFT pathway for England (first published February 2023; March 2026 version checked October 2026) lists red flags that, with back or leg pain of recent onset or getting worse, need emergency referral to hospital for MRI:
- difficulty starting to pass urine or reduced sensation of urine flow;
- altered sensation around the anus, perineum or genitals (the S2–S5 'saddle' dermatomes);
- severe or progressive weakness in both legs;
- loss of the sensation of rectal fullness;
- new sexual dysfunction.
The pathway asks for MRI as soon as possible and within four hours of the request, and it warns that a bladder scan alone should not decide whether to scan. Radicular pain in both legs without these features needs urgent (not emergency) assessment and safety-netting. The sacral roots carry bladder, bowel and sexual function and saddle sensation, which is why these symptoms matter.

