In a displaced femoral neck fracture the shaft is no longer held by the neck. Muscles crossing the hip, including the gluteals, hamstrings, adductors and rectus femoris, pull the distal fragment upwards and shorten the leg. Iliopsoas, attached to the lesser trochanter, and the short lateral rotators attached around the greater trochanter turn the shaft outwards, so the leg lies externally rotated.

The adult femoral head is supplied mainly by retinacular branches of the medial circumflex femoral artery, which run up the neck beneath the synovial reflection of the capsule. The artery of the ligament of the head, from the obturator artery, contributes little in adults. A displaced intracapsular fracture is likely to tear the retinacular vessels, so the head is at high risk of avascular necrosis and non-union if it is fixed. NICE CG124 therefore recommends replacement arthroplasty (hemiarthroplasty or total hip replacement) for displaced intracapsular fractures, with total hip replacement considered for selected people who were independent before the fracture, are fit for the operation and are likely to benefit beyond two years. Extracapsular fractures keep the head's blood supply and are fixed instead, usually with a sliding hip screw for trochanteric fractures.

The injection probably damaged the sciatic nerve (L4–S3). It leaves the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen below piriformis and passes through the lower, medial part of the buttock between the greater trochanter and the ischial tuberosity. The expected deficits are:
- weak knee flexion from loss of the hamstrings;
- paralysis of all muscles below the knee, through both the common fibular nerve (dorsiflexion and eversion, giving foot drop) and the tibial nerve (plantarflexion and inversion), producing a flail foot;
- sensory loss over the sole, the dorsum of the foot and the lateral leg, with the medial leg spared because it is supplied by the saphenous branch of the femoral nerve;
- an absent ankle jerk. Knee extension and the knee jerk (femoral nerve) and hip adduction (obturator nerve) are preserved.

To avoid this, gluteal injections are given into the upper outer quadrant of the buttock, well above and lateral to the nerve's path, or at the ventrogluteal site in gluteus medius, defined using the anterior superior iliac spine, iliac tubercle and greater trochanter, which lies away from major nerves and vessels.
